Let~$K_n$ be the complete graph with vertex set~$V=\{v_1,\dots,v_n\}$, and let~$q:=v_n$. Its reduced Laplacian~$	ilde{L}$ is the matrix~$nI_{n-1}-J_{n-1}$ where~$J_{n-1}$ is the~$(n-1)	imes(n-1)$ matrix whose entries are all~$1$. The inverse is~$	ilde{L}^{-1}= rac{1}{n}(I_{n-1}+J_{n-1})$, and therefore,~$\mathrm{ord}_{q}(v)=n$ for all vertices~$v
eq q$. To reduce~$	ilde{L}$ to Hermite normal form, add columns~$2$ through~$n-1$ to the first column of~$	ilde{L}$, then add the first column to each of the others. The result is the matrix formed by replacing the first column of~$nI_{n-1}$ by a column of all~$1$s. So the image of~$	ilde{L}$ in~$(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^{n-1}$ is spanned by the vector of all~$1$s. See Figure~ef{fig: ps-complete} for an example.  egin{figure}[ht] \centering  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]  oreach \x in {0,...,4} { 
